  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Compute linear transformation Z = X W and zero out rows whose Euclidean L2 norm falls strictly below a gate threshold.\n\nNorm-gating imposes dynamic sparsity by pruning low-energy feature activations.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nZ = X W, \\quad Z'_i = \\begin{cases} Z_i & \\|Z_i\\|_2 \\ge \\tau \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & \\|Z_i\\|_2 < \\tau \\end{cases}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute matrix multiplication Z = X W\n2. Evaluate Euclidean L2 norm for each output row vector\n3. Zero out rows failing the norm threshold criterion\n\n### Returns\nNested list representing gated transformation matrix.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Norm-Gated Linear Transform\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nLinear algebra is the foundational mathematical language of machine learning. Data samples, image pixels, and word embeddings are all represented as multidimensional vectors and matrices. **Norm-Gated Linear Transform** transforms coordinate systems, extracts principal axes of variation, or decomposes complex transformations into simpler geometric building blocks.\n\nNorm-gating imposes dynamic sparsity by pruning low-energy feature activations. In this problem, the goal is to: compute linear transformation z = x w and zero out rows whose euclidean l2 norm falls strictly below a gate threshold.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nZ = X W, \\quad Z'_i = \\begin{cases} Z_i & \\|Z_i\\|_2 \\ge \\tau \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & \\|Z_i\\|_2 < \\tau \\end{cases}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute matrix multiplication Z = X W\n2. **Phase 2**: Evaluate Euclidean L2 norm for each output row vector\n3. **Phase 3**: Zero out rows failing the norm threshold criterion\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Feature Compression & Projections**: Operations like low-rank approximations and orthogonal projections compress high-dimensional feature spaces while preserving maximum variance.\n* **Numerical Stability**: When inverting matrices or solving linear systems, ill-conditioned matrices (matrices with very small determinants or high condition numbers) require regularization to prevent numerical instability.\n* **Vectorized Execution**: Expressing computations as matrix multiplications allows GPUs and CPUs to leverage hardware tensor cores for maximum throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: X = [[1.0, 1.0], [0.1, 0.1]], W = [[2.0, 0.0], [0.0, 2.0]], threshold = 1.0\n\nOutput: [[2.0, 2.0], [0.0, 0.0]]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated numpy_norm_gate under standard test conditions."
